# November 19, 2026: Lamoille County and Waterbury, Vermont (region vtc, Central Vermont).
# Drawn at random by tools/choose.py. Stowe, Morristown, Hyde Park, Johnson, Wolcott, Elmore and Cambridge in Lamoille
# County, and the Waterbury leads left by the Washington County batch (vtc_kaqi): the old mill village, the seminary and
# the state hospital. Covered bridges, a mill village, meeting houses, a courthouse, a CCC park and an asylum.
# Facts come from Wikipedia articles read through tools/wiki_raw.py (each summarising its National Register form), the
# National Register forms for Moscow Village and the Vermont State Hospital read through tools/page.py, the Waterbury
# walking-tour leaflet, the Vermont Historical Society museum directory, VTDigger, and the Vermont State Parks page.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'vtc_txwo','label':'Lamoille County and Waterbury, Vermont','added':'2026-11-19 06:00'}
def HM(n,t): return ['HMdb: '+t,'https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=%d'%n]
def NR(ref,t): return ['National Register form: '+t,'https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/%s_text'%ref]
WTOUR=['Town of Waterbury: historic walking tour (PDF)','https://www.waterburyvt.com/fileadmin/files/Community_Resources_files/WalkingTour.pdf']
LVRT=['Vermont Rail Trails: Lamoille Valley Rail Trail','https://railtrails.vermont.gov/trails/lamoille-valley-rail-trail/']
PLACES=[
# ---- Stowe ----
N('gold-brook-covered-bridge','Gold Brook Covered Bridge (Emily\'s Bridge)','Stowe','vtc','landmark','standing',44.4404,-72.67986,'exact','public','drive','','1844–',
  'A small one-lane covered bridge carrying Covered Bridge Road over Gold Brook in Stowe Hollow, built about 1844 by John W. Smith. It is the only surviving timber Howe truss in Vermont on a public road, an early one too (the truss was patented in 1840), and the only covered bridge left in Stowe; in 1969 the town voted to maintain it for good. It is known as Emily\'s Bridge after a ghost story that seems to date from the late 1960s or early 1970s; no record of any such death there has been found.',
  'A 48.5-foot single span on dry-laid stone abutments, with vertical board siding stopping short of the eaves, a metal roof and a plank deck. The Howe truss timbers are visible inside. Open to traffic.',
  'Vermont\'s best-known "haunted" bridge, and why the story is probably a high-school paper.',
  'A town road bridge, open to traffic and free. On Covered Bridge Road just north of its junction with Gold Brook Road and Stowe Hollow Road; no mapped lot, so pull well off the narrow road and watch for cars in the one lane. Private houses nearby.',
  [W('Gold Brook Covered Bridge'),HM(261353,'Gold Brook Covered Bridge'),['Vermont Historical Society: Emily\'s Bridge','https://vermonthistory.org/emilys-bridge']],brick='no'),
N('seaver-sawmill-moscow','Seaver sawmill and dam, Moscow','Stowe','vtc','industry','standing',44.44081,-72.7161,'site','view','drive','','1822–',
  'Alexander Seaver dammed the Little River in the 1820s at what was then Smith\'s Falls, south of Stowe village, and built a sawmill, an iron forge for box stoves, a house and a barn; a grist mill was added about 1840. From 1836 the Smith family ran the site and added a starch factory, sash, door and blind works and woodworking shops. The village took the name Moscow from a broken mill saw hung up as a bell and nicknamed "the Bell of Moscow". The mill is the centre of the Moscow Village Historic District, listed in 2008.',
  'The 1½-story saw and grist mill on the north bank, on a massive fieldstone foundation about 6 feet thick with stone arches in its walls, and the dam beside it: about 200 feet long and 11 feet high, timber and rock of 1822 rebuilt in concrete in 1918, with "1918" cast in the concrete and old timbers visible in the sluiceway (Register form, 2008). Interior not checked. The 1886 general store across the road was demolished after it closed in 2016 and rebuilt to the old design (VTDigger, 2025).',
  '',
  'Privately owned (the Register form names the owners; a machine shop was in the old woodworking building then). Look from Moscow Road and the 1949 Moscow Road bridge over the Little River; do not go onto the property or the dam. No mapped parking.',
  [W('Moscow Village Historic District'),NR('08000744','Moscow Village Historic District'),['VTDigger: new Stowe exhibit explores history of Moscow village (2025)','https://vtdigger.org/2025/10/22/new-stowe-exhibit-explores-history-of-moscow-village/']],brick='no'),
# ---- Morristown ----
N('red-covered-bridge-morristown','Red Covered Bridge','Morristown','vtc','landmark','standing',44.51859,-72.67773,'exact','public','drive','','1896–',
  'A queen post covered bridge carrying Cole Hill Road over Sterling Brook, built in 1896: the only 19th-century covered bridge left in Morristown and one of the last built in Vermont in the historic period. Its bottom chords are unusually thick, made up of twelve layers of planking. Steel beams were put under the deck in 1971 to carry the traffic.',
  'A 64-foot single span with red-painted board siding and a red metal roof; the north abutment sits partly on a big granite outcrop. Open to one lane of traffic.',
  '',
  'A town road bridge, free, in a rural area just north of the junction of Cole Hill Road, Sterling Valley Road and Moren Loop. No mapped lot: pull off the road with care.',
  [W('Red Covered Bridge (Morristown, Vermont)')],brick='no'),
N('noyes-house-museum','Noyes House Museum','Morrisville','vtc','civic','standing',44.56128,-72.60115,'exact','open','drive','','1797–',
  'John Safford built the house in 1797 above the Lamoille River; his son Jedediah, who bought it in 1810, added a brick Federal-style wing and built an English barn in the 1820s. It was the first place settled in what became Morrisville village. The Morristown Historical Society runs it as a museum of 19th-century life in the town.',
  'Seventeen rooms and a carriage barn of furniture, quilts, costumes, tools, military objects, toys and Toby mugs; the collection includes an 18th-century Native dugout canoe and a large set of hairwork. The house and barn themselves are the oldest things in the village.',
  '',
  'Morristown Historical Society, 122 Lower Main Street. The Vermont Historical Society\'s museum directory gives summer hours of Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday 12 to 3, and in the school year Tuesday and Saturday 12 to 3 and Thursday 1 to 4, or by appointment; admission by donation. The directory warns its hours may be out of date: check with the museum. Municipal lots on Hutchins Street and Richmond Street, a few minutes\' walk.',
  [W('Noyes House Museum'),['Vermont Historical Society: Lamoille County museum directory','https://vermonthistory.org/lamoille-county-museum-directory'],HM(155274,'Noyes House Museum')],
  th=[44.56259,-72.59765],thn='Hutchins Street lot',brick='?'),
N('peoples-academy-grout-observatory','Peoples Academy, Grout Observatory and bandshell','Morrisville','vtc','civic','standing',44.56283,-72.59015,'site','view','drive','','1927–',
  'The town high school on the hill at the east end of Main Street, given with its land by Alexander Hamilton Copley, a Lamoille County native who made his money in pharmaceuticals in Boston; he had bought the land for a house for himself and his wife, who died first. The Classical Revival building is nearly a copy of a high school in Provincetown, Massachusetts. George C. Grout gave a round brick astronomical observatory in 1930, and the wooden bandshell of the same year is where graduations are held.',
  'The long two-story red brick school with limestone trim, a balustrade with a clock over the centre and Corinthian columns at the door; the small circular brick observatory and the bandshell on the grounds. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working public high school: see it from Copley Avenue and the public ways, and stay off the grounds during school hours. Whether the observatory is ever open to the public was not checked.',
  [W('Peoples Academy')],brick='yes'),
# ---- Hyde Park ----
N('lamoille-county-courthouse','Lamoille County Courthouse','Hyde Park','vtc','civic','standing',44.5931,-72.61737,'exact','view','drive','','1911–',
  'Hyde Park was made the shire town when the county was formed, and its first courthouse went up in 1836. That building and 17 others burned in the village\'s Great Fire of 1910; the county records came through in fireproof safes. The present courthouse was built in 1911-12 to a design by Burlington architect Zachary Taylor Austin, a mix of Colonial Revival and Romanesque Revival not found in Vermont\'s other courthouses.',
  'A 2½-story brick building with a corner clock tower under a pyramid roof, a columned porch with "Union Jack" balustrades and round-arched upper windows. The interior, with a courtroom across the whole second floor, had seen little change by the 1995 Register form, which also says the fire vaults may be those of the 1836 courthouse. Interior not checked for visitors.',
  '',
  'County building at 154 Main Street, set back from the road with other government buildings. See it from Main Street; whether and when the public may go in was not checked. A mapped lot on Church Street is about 150 m north.',
  [W('Lamoille County Courthouse')],th=[44.59438,-72.6171],thn='Church Street lot',brick='?'),
# ---- Johnson ----
N('power-house-covered-bridge','Power House Covered Bridge','Johnson','vtc','landmark','standing',44.63608,-72.67041,'exact','public','drive','','1870–',
  'A queen post covered bridge over the Gihon River on School Street, named for a hydroelectric station that was later built just upstream and is now gone. Wikipedia gives it as built in 1870 and, in its opening line, 1872. Heavy snow brought the roof down in 2000 and the side walls fell into the river; the deck, already on steel beams, survived, and the bridge was rebuilt and reopened on June 29, 2002.',
  'A 63.5-foot single span on stone abutments faced with concrete, with iron rods in the trusses and a metal roof. Much of what stands is the 2002 rebuilding.',
  '',
  'A town road bridge on School Street just west of VT 100C, east of Johnson village; open to traffic and free. No mapped lot.',
  [W('Power House Covered Bridge')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('scribner-covered-bridge','Scribner Covered Bridge','Johnson','vtc','landmark','standing',44.63814,-72.64857,'exact','public','drive','','',
  'Also called the Mudgett Covered Bridge, it carries Rocky Road over the Gihon River in East Johnson. Its builder and date are not known. It started as an open pony truss bridge and was covered later, one of only a few Vermont covered bridges made that way, which is why its queen post trusses are so low and have no inner bracing.',
  'A 48-foot single span with board siding and a metal roof, on concrete abutments of 1960; the short trusses have metal plates at the corner joints.',
  '',
  'A town road bridge on Rocky Road between VT 100C and Sinclair Road; one lane, free. No mapped lot; private houses nearby.',
  [W('Scribner Covered Bridge')],brick='no'),
# ---- Wolcott ----
N('fisher-covered-railroad-bridge','Fisher Covered Railroad Bridge','Wolcott','vtc','rail','standing',44.53231,-72.42773,'exact','public','walk','stations','1908–',
  'Built in 1908 by the Boston and Maine Railroad for the St. Johnsbury and Lamoille County line over the Lamoille River. When it was listed in 1974 it was the last covered bridge in the United States still carrying trains. In 1968, when it was to be replaced with a steel truss, a pier and four steel girders were put under it instead. It now carries the Lamoille Valley Rail Trail.',
  'A two-span doubled Town lattice truss, 103.5 feet long, with a raised monitor running along the roof (for engine smoke), board siding with a skirt below, and stone abutments capped in concrete. A rare doubled lattice in Vermont.',
  'The last covered bridge in America to carry trains.',
  'On the Lamoille Valley Rail Trail, a state rail trail, south of VT 15 and southeast of Wolcott village; a small mapped lot off VT 15 is just north of the bridge. The trail page could not be read in this session (HTTP 403): hours and rules not checked.',
  [W('Fisher Covered Railroad Bridge'),HM(36934,'Fisher Bridge'),LVRT],th=[44.53288,-72.42757],thn='Lot off VT 15',brick='no'),
# ---- Elmore ----
N('elmore-state-park-ccc','Elmore State Park CCC beach house and fire tower','Elmore','vtc','civic','standing',44.54177,-72.52833,'exact','public','drive','','1934–',
  'In 1934 the town of Elmore and some of its people gave the state 30 acres at the north end of Lake Elmore, and the Civilian Conservation Corps built the access road, the beach and the beach house in 1934-36, using adjacent federal land as well. The state took over the federal part in 1938 and built the observation tower on Elmore Mountain in 1939. The CCC work was listed on the National Register in 2002; Wikipedia says the remains of the CCC camp are also in the area.',
  'The CCC beach house, still in use with a function room, restrooms, changing rooms and a concession stand, and the fire tower on the 2,608-foot summit, reached by two trails. Where the CCC camp remains are was not found. The park page said the tower would be closed from September 14 to October 30 for repairs, with the trailhead lot and trails closed on certain days in late September; check its status before going.',
  '',
  'Vermont State Parks (Department of Forests, Parks and Recreation). Open from the Friday of Memorial Day weekend to the second Monday in October; day-use and camping fees. Pets are not allowed on the beach at any time. Beach lot off Beach Road from VT 12; a separate trailhead lot serves the mountain trails.',
  [W('Elmore State Park'),['Vermont State Parks: Elmore','https://vtstateparks.com/elmore.html']],th=[44.54246,-72.52831],thn='Beach Road lot',brick='?'),
# ---- Cambridge ----
N('cambridge-meetinghouse','Cambridge Meetinghouse (Old Brick Church)','Jeffersonville','vtc','worship','standing',44.6436,-72.83134,'exact','view','drive','','1826–',
  'Built in 1826 as a union church to be shared by four denominations, which made it a scene of sectarian quarrels; the Perfectionist movement that led to the Oneida Community in New York in the 1840s rose here. The town began holding meetings in it in 1866, the last services were held in 1889, and it was the town hall until 1958. It now holds the post office.',
  'A two-story red brick Federal meeting house with "1826" over the door (Wikimedia photograph), a pedimented gable and projecting centre bay, a recessed entrance framed by pilasters, and a square tower with an octagonal belfry and cupola. A stage was added at the back in the early 1900s. The post office lobby is the part the public can enter in its hours; the rest of the interior not checked.',
  '',
  '85 Church Street (VT 108) in Jeffersonville village, next to the Smugglers Notch Inn; the post office keeps postal hours. Street and lot parking alongside.',
  [W('Cambridge Meetinghouse')],th=[44.64361,-72.83105],thn='Church Street lot',brick='yes'),
N('grist-mill-covered-bridge','Grist Mill Covered Bridge','Cambridge','vtc','landmark','standing',44.63665,-72.82534,'exact','public','drive','','1800s–',
  'Also called the Scott, Bryant or Canyon Covered Bridge, it carries Canyon Road over the Brewster River just off VT 108 south of Jeffersonville, and stood near a grist mill that gave it its name. It is one of nine Burr arch truss bridges documented in Vermont and one of three covered bridges left in Cambridge; the date it was built is given only as the 19th century. It was rehabilitated in 2004 and reopened on October 13 that year.',
  'An 84.5-foot single span with Burr arches of single bolted timbers either side of the post-and-brace trusses, board siding with two window cutouts on each side, a plank deck and a metal roof.',
  '',
  'A town road bridge on Canyon Road a short way east of VT 108; one lane, free. No mapped lot.',
  [W('Grist Mill Covered Bridge')],brick='no'),
N('poland-covered-bridge','Poland Covered Bridge (Cambridge Junction)','Cambridge','vtc','landmark','standing',44.6511,-72.8146,'exact','public','drive','','1887–',
  'A 152.5-foot Burr arch bridge over the Lamoille River, built in 1887 by George W. Holmes. It is named for Judge Poland, who pushed the town into building it; the Morrisville paper said in 1888 that taxpayers would call it "the Bridge of Sighs". Poland died in his hayfield the next July. A truck hit a king post in 1995 and the structure began to fail; it was rebuilt and reopened on July 4, 2004.',
  'Long Burr trusses with laminated arches, siding that follows the curve of the arches at the centre, and stone abutments capped in concrete. A state historical marker and the Cambridge Rail Era markers (HMdb) stand at the bridge.',
  '',
  'On Cambridge Junction Road between VT 15 and VT 109, east of Jeffersonville; one lane, free. A small mapped lot is just south of the bridge.',
  [W('Poland Covered Bridge'),HM(94319,'Cambridge Junction Bridge'),HM(207875,'Cambridge Rail Era History I')],th=[44.65063,-72.81425],thn='Lot at the south end of the bridge',brick='no'),
# ---- Waterbury ----
N('waterbury-feed-company-mill','Waterbury Feed Company mill, Mill Village','Waterbury','vtc','industry','standing',44.34212,-72.74804,'exact','view','drive','','1830s–',
  'Mill Village on Stowe Street was for most of the 19th century an industrial neighbourhood powered by three dams on Graves (Thatcher) Brook. The grist and feed mill is the only mill building left; the first mill on the site went up in 1807. Wikipedia dates the present mill, dam and penstock to about 1830, and the town walking tour says the mill was built around 1835. Interstate 89\'s Exit 10 ramps now pass high over the south end of the village, partly on old mill ground.',
  'The mill building at 92 Stowe Street; the remains of the dam and penstock are behind it (walking tour). OpenStreetMap lists a restaurant at that address now (not checked). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private building on Stowe Street in the Mill Village Historic District, listed in 1979. See it from the street; the dam is behind the building and not on a public way. Street parking on Stowe Street.',
  [W('Mill Village Historic District (Waterbury, Vermont)'),WTOUR],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('green-mountain-seminary','Green Mountain Seminary','Waterbury Center','vtc','civic','standing',44.37826,-72.71872,'exact','view','drive','','1869–',
  'Built in 1869 as a coeducational Free Will Baptist school, with a south addition of 1871; the lower floors held classrooms and a chapel and the top floor and attic housed the men. After a run of school and boarding uses it was deeded to the town in 1895 as a graded school. From 1947 to 1952 the Green Mountain Horology society met here and restored the Riley Whiting tall clock.',
  'A large T-shaped Italianate frame building on a high stone foundation, with quoined corners, paired brackets at the eaves and scrolled hoods over the doors; the cupola was taken off in 1941. As of 2009 it held the Waterbury Center library and a day care centre (Wikipedia); current use not checked. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  '201 Hollow Road, on a rise just north of the main crossroads of Waterbury Center. See it from Hollow Road; who uses it now and when it is open were not checked.',
  [W('Green Mountain Seminary')],brick='no'),
N('vermont-state-hospital','Vermont State Hospital (Waterbury State Office Complex)','Waterbury','vtc','inst','standing',44.33207,-72.75334,'site','view','drive','','1891–2011',
  'The state asylum opened in 1891 to relieve crowding at the Brattleboro asylum; its main building, designed by Rand & Taylor, was finished in 1896. Patient numbers peaked at 1,728 in the mid-1930s under superintendent Eugene Stanley, an advocate of eugenics and forced sterilization, and "Waterbury" became local slang for madness. From 1963 empty wards became state offices. Tropical Storm Irene flooded the campus in 2011 and the hospital closed; some buildings were removed and the rest reopened as the Waterbury State Office Complex in 2015.',
  'The long red-brick main hospital of 1891-96 on a high granite base, with four round two-story ward buildings under conical roofs, a 3½-story centre building, towers, cupolas and porches, set at the head of a horseshoe drive and green; a brick smokestack with "V.S.H." in the brickwork is seen from I-89 and much of the town (Register form, 2016). The interiors are 2015 offices.',
  'The asylum whose name became a Vermont insult, and what became of it after Irene.',
  'State office complex at the south end of Waterbury village (Register district, 2016). See it from the drives and the public roads; it is a working state office, and access to the buildings was not checked.',
  [W('Vermont State Hospital'),NR('16000765','Vermont State Hospital Historic District')],brick='yes'),
N('waterbury-white-meeting-house','White Meeting House (Waterbury Congregational Church)','Waterbury','vtc','worship','standing',44.33807,-72.75738,'exact','view','drive','','1824–',
  'Waterbury\'s United Church of Christ, known as the White Meeting House, was built in 1824 by the Carpenter brothers, who also built the town\'s 1826 Stagecoach Inn. It was remodelled in 1860 to give it a Gothic look; the chapel was added in 1880 and the stained glass in 1890. Hope Cemetery behind it holds many of the village\'s early settlers.',
  'A three-bay frame church with buttresses ending in pinnacles at the corners and a two-tier tower and spire over the double doors; the 1890 stained glass windows. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'An active church at 8 North Main Street (US 2), in the Waterbury Village Historic District. See it from the street and the cemetery; services and opening times not checked. Lots on Elm Street and Parker Court nearby.',
  [WTOUR,W('Waterbury Village Historic District')],th=[44.33679,-72.75617],thn='Elm Street lot',brick='no'),
]
